<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Alexis Brialmont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henri Alexis Brialmont was a Belgian military engineer and general, the leading fortress designer of his age. He planned the fortifications of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> as the national redoubt and, in the 1880s, the rings of concrete forts around Liège and Namur. He also worked on defences for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. The Liège forts delayed the German advance in August 1914 before falling to heavy siege artillery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-alexis-brialmont.jpg" alt="Henri Alexis Brialmont" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_Brialmont.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Brialmont was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a>, in Limburg, on 25 May 1821. The whole of Limburg was then claimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> after the Belgian Revolution, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of 1839 divided it. His father, Mathieu Brialmont, had been a captain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s Grande Armée, served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and then the Belgian army, and became Minister of War in 1850. A poor pupil, Henri-Alexis still entered a military academy, and in 1859 he married the daughter of Louis de Potter, a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was commissioned in the engineers in 1843 and rose quickly: private secretary to the war minister, General Baron Félix Chazal, from 1847 to 1850, then the staff corps, colonel in 1868, major-general in 1874, when he took charge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>'s fortifications, and lieutenant general in 1877. He ended as Inspector-General of the Army, retiring in 1892. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> he was chief of staff of the Army of Antwerp, but he never served in a war.</p><p>He campaigned throughout his life for a larger army and navy, which brought him into conflict with Walthère Frère-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orban">Orban</a> and the Liberals, and he argued for universal military service in place of the system of paid replacements. Leopold, Duke of Brabant, the future King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Leopold II</a>, shared his ideas, and Brialmont backed colonial expansion and helped to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, though he declined to become the king's aide-de-camp. He founded the newspaper La Belgique Militaire in 1872 and wrote many books on fortification.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Brialmont held that forts had to stand several miles outside a city to keep long-range artillery away from it; his stood on average 4 kilometres out, in a ring of concrete works with retractable gun cupolas, and he urged wire entanglements against infantry as early as 1879. Twelve forts were built at Liège and nine at Namur between 1887 and 1892. In 1882 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Carol I of Romania</a> commissioned him, as a private citizen, to design a ring of 18 forts around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, built from 1883 to 1900; the roads linking them became the basis of the city's ring road.</p><p>Brialmont died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 21 July 1903. His forts were at the heart of the Belgian defence in 1914, at Liège, Namur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, but gave way to German 420 mm and Austrian 305 mm howitzers. Some were upgraded and fought again in May 1940.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henri Alexis Brialmont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its main works were fortifications: the national redoubt of Antwerp, built from the 1850s, and the ring forts of Liège and Namur designed by <b>Henri Alexis Brialmont</b> in the 1880s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venlo">Venlo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Saint-Josse-ten-Noode, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.37,6.17&amp;year=1874" aria-label="Venlo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4690 385 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henri Alexis Brialmont"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5140 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5140 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.37,6.17&amp;year=1874">What was here in 1874? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
